{"id":"book-v-translator-and-historical-edition-notes-part-23","label":"Book V. Wherein Tertullian proves, with respect to St. Paul's epistles, what he had proved in the preceding book with respect to St. Luke's gospel. Far from being at variance, they were in perfect unison with the writings of the Old Testament, and therefore testified that the Creator was the only God, and that the Lord Jesus was his Christ. As in the preceding books, Tertullian supports his argument with profound reasoning, and many happy illustrations of Holy Scripture. — Translator and historical edition notes (part 23)","headingNoteRefs":"","context":"Book V. Wherein Tertullian proves, with respect to St. Paul's epistles, what he had proved in the preceding book with respect to St. Luke's gospel. Far from being at variance, they were in perfect unison with the writings of the Old Testament, and therefore testified that the Creator was the only God, and that the Lord Jesus was his Christ. As in the preceding books, Tertullian supports his argument with profound reasoning, and many happy illustrations of Holy Scripture.","kind":"commentary","blocks":[{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3643]: Eph. i. 13 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3643"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3644]: Joel ii. 28 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3644"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3645]: Eph. ii. 17 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3645"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3646]: Ps. xxiv. 10 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3646"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3647]: Eph. i. 17 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3647"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3648]: Isa. xi. 2 .","kind":"commentary","noteId":"tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3648"},{"text":"[^tertullian-against-marcion-v-iv-3649]: Eph. i. 